**Theorem 4.4 (A negative atom for a certificate matching the QJM port).**
No globally product-positive certificate of Definition 4.1 can have
value $\lambda_Q$. More explicitly, complementarity at the QJM point
would force $A_N^+=vv^T$, all other event multipliers zero, and

$$
b_{\mathrm B,0}^TB_y(u_+)b_{\mathrm B,0}
=-\frac{19}{130}gn(u_+^Tv)^2<0,
\tag{4.14}
$$

where $v$ is the positive unit eigenvector of $C_Q$.

Proof. If a globally valid certificate had value $\lambda_Q$, QJM's
slack identity at $S_Q$ would have zero total nonnegative slack.
All other signed event inequalities are strict by (2.14), while
the positive $N$ inequality has a one-dimensional kernel. The unit
trace normalization (4.2) therefore forces the asserted event
multipliers. The positive-weight B generator in QJM equation (4.4)
must also have zero atom slack:
$b_{\mathrm B,0}^TB_y(a_{\mathrm B,0})b_{\mathrm B,0}=0$.
For a fixed $b$ and unit preparations, the TP term $b^TY_yb$ is
unchanged. Changing the B preparation to $u_+$ increases its event
payoff by exactly (2.25). Subtracting those equal TP terms in (4.4)
proves (4.14), contradicting positivity on the full independent-product
domain. Independently, the legal port of Theorem 2.2 has smaller error,
so no universal lower at $\lambda_Q$ can hold.
This excludes matching that specific sufficient point. QJM makes no
optimality assertion, and none of its stated bounds is refuted.
$\square$

